    <main class="main-area">
        <header class="ma-hd">
            <h1 class="container ma-hd__ttl">INTRODUCTION</h1>
        </header>
        <div class="container">
            <nav class="bc">
                <span xmlns:v="http://rdf.data-vocabulary.org/#">
                    <span class="bc__root" typeof="v:Breadcrumb"><a href="../" rel="v:url" property="v:title">HOME</a> ＞
                        <span class="bc__last">INTRODUCTION</span></span>
                </span>
            </nav>
            <div class="content-area">
                <section class="ct-sec ct-sec__intro">
                    <div class="row">
                        <div class="col-sm-6">
                            <p>Around 1300 years ago, two sculptors carved two Kannon statues out of a sacred tree. One
                                of them was enshrined here at Hasedera in Kamakura. It is commonly called “Eleven-headed
                                Kannon” since it has eleven heads on top of its own head. It measures 9.18 meters tall
                                and this makes it one of the largest wooden Buddhist statues in Japan.<br>Hesedera
                                temple resides on a mountain called “Kannon-zan”. The ground holds a large variety of
                                flowers which bloom throughout the year welcoming visitors. Additionally, the scenic
                                view from the observation platform on the upper ground is recognized as one of the most
                                picturesque spots in Kamakura.</p>
                        </div>
                        <div class="col-sm-6 ct-sec__right">
                            <img src="//www.hasedera.jp/common/img/intro-01.jpg" alt="">
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="row">
                        <div class="col-sm-6">
                            <img src="//www.hasedera.jp/common/img/intro-02.jpg" alt="" class="mb20-xs">
                        </div>
                        <div class="col-sm-6">
                            <p><span class="b">History of Hasedera</span><br>According to the legend, in 721 AD, two
                                Kannon statues were carved out of one sacred tree in Hatsuse (in the present day Nara
                                Prefecture). One was enshrined at the Hasedera temple in Nara, the other was thrown into
                                the ocean with a prayer to make it reappear and save people elsewhere. After the Kannon
                                statue traveled across the waters for fifteen long years, it finally washed ashore in a
                                place in the present day Kanagawa Prefecture. In 736 AD, the Hasedera temple in Kamakura
                                was established to enshrine the Kannon statue. </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="row ct-sec__intro__thumbs">
                        <div class="col-sm-3"><img src="//www.hasedera.jp/common/img/intro-03s.jpg" alt=""></div>
                        <div class="col-sm-3"><img src="//www.hasedera.jp/common/img/intro-04s.jpg" alt=""></div>
                        <div class="col-sm-3"><img src="//www.hasedera.jp/common/img/intro-05s.jpg" alt=""></div>
                        <div class="col-sm-3"><img src="//www.hasedera.jp/common/img/intro-06s.jpg" alt=""></div>
                    </div>
                </section>

